<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RUT_Tarred_Hediffs.xml                UtinniPatches — SUMP_TAR_NASTINESS_1

  Item spec §2, "tarred-pawn hediffs — tar on yourself: move/work penalties,
  mood, hygiene-flavored stink, spreads filth, maybe flammability. Severity
  from exposure; removal is not free."

  Given out by RM_MapComponent_CarriedFilthHediffLink the moment a pawn's
  own Pawn_FilthTracker starts carrying RM_Filth_Tar (RUT_Sump.xml's own
  RM_CarriedFilthHediffExtension wiring), and its severity is then driven
  entirely by whether that pawn is STILL carrying it — the comp below,
  RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.HediffCompProperties_CarriedFilthExposure
  — reading Pawn_FilthTracker.CarriedFilthListForReading directly, the same
  field SUMP_WALKWAYS_1's own tracking mechanism already fills. No second
  tar representation: this hediff is a read-only passenger on that list.

  "spreads filth" (the spec's own line) needs no mechanism of its own —
  Pawn_FilthTracker.Notify_EnteredNewCell (RimWorld/Pawn_FilthTracker.cs)
  already drops carried filth onto clean cells the carrier crosses, purely
  from carrying RM_Filth_Tar at all; a tarred pawn is, structurally, already
  a walking source of more tar filth. This hediff's own job is the pawn-
  facing HALF: what carrying it costs the pawn themselves.

  hediffClass HediffWithComps (stated explicitly — HediffDef.hediffClass
  defaults to plain Hediff, which carries no comps at all and would make the
  <comps> block below load clean and do nothing, same trap
  RUT_Staggerseed_Hediffs.xml's own header already flags).

  Three stages, thresholds 0 / 0.35 / 0.7 — same threshold shape
  RUT_VorrelBrood already uses in this repo for a severity-driven escalation
  read at a glance. Move penalty via capMods (Moving), work penalty via
  statFactors (WorkSpeedGlobal, RimWorld/StatDefOf.cs — confirmed real,
  a Factor-type stat so a multiplicative statFactor is the correct field,
  not statOffsets), flammability via statOffsets (StatModifier list,
  confirmed on HediffStage — Verse/HediffStage.cs:92) once a pawn is
  properly slicked, not from the first light tracking. No pain, no capacity
  offsets beyond Moving — this is squalor and hazard, not an injury.

  "Removal is not free" (the spec's own line): severityPerDayClean below
  heals a tarred pawn off on their own once their boots are clean, but
  RUT_ScrubTarredSolvent (this item's piece 3, RUT_Tarred_Solvents.xml) is
  the deliberate, priced cure via a real Recipe_RemoveHediff bill — same
  shape RSW_RemoveBrainWorm already ships in this repo.
-->
<Defs>

  <HediffDef>
    <defName>RUT_Tarred</defName>
    <label>tarred</label>
    <description>Black tar tracked in and stuck fast — on boots, cuffs, skin. It does not wash off with water, it reeks, and it does not care whose floor it ends up on next. Left alone it thickens; scrubbed clean, or dissolved with the right solvent, it lets go.</description>
    <hediffClass>HediffWithComps</hediffClass>
    <defaultLabelColor>(0.14, 0.12, 0.10)</defaultLabelColor>
    <maxSeverity>1</maxSeverity>
    <isBad>true</isBad>
    <scenarioCanAdd>false</scenarioCanAdd>
    <comps>
      <li Class="RimMandrake.EnvironmentalHazards.HediffCompProperties_CarriedFilthExposure" MayRequire="mandrake.rm.environmentalhazards">
        <filthDef>RM_Filth_Tar</filthDef>
        <!-- INVENTED-BUILD tuning: ~1.1 in-game days from fresh contact to the
             top stage while continuously re-exposed; ~1.7 days to heal off
             clean on its own. SUMP_TAR_NASTINESS_1's own tuning law ("full
             cleanliness should be a losing fight") is why clean-healing is
             slower than dirty-accruing, not faster — avoidance works, but
             slowly, next to a priced solvent. -->
        <severityPerDayCarrying>0.9</severityPerDayCarrying>
        <severityPerDayClean>-0.6</severityPerDayClean>
      </li>
    </comps>
    <stages>
      <li>
        <label>tracked in tar</label>
        <capMods>
          <li>
            <capacity>Moving</capacity>
            <postFactor>0.92</postFactor>
          </li>
        </capMods>
        <statFactors>
          <WorkSpeedGlobal>0.95</WorkSpeedGlobal>
        </statFactors>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>tar-slicked</label>
        <minSeverity>0.35</minSeverity>
        <capMods>
          <li>
            <capacity>Moving</capacity>
            <postFactor>0.8</postFactor>
          </li>
        </capMods>
        <statFactors>
          <WorkSpeedGlobal>0.85</WorkSpeedGlobal>
        </statFactors>
        <statOffsets>
          <Flammability>0.25</Flammability>
        </statOffsets>
      </li>
      <li>
        <label>tar-caked</label>
        <minSeverity>0.7</minSeverity>
        <capMods>
          <li>
            <capacity>Moving</capacity>
            <postFactor>0.65</postFactor>
          </li>
        </capMods>
        <statFactors>
          <WorkSpeedGlobal>0.7</WorkSpeedGlobal>
        </statFactors>
        <statOffsets>
          <Flammability>0.5</Flammability>
        </statOffsets>
      </li>
    </stages>
  </HediffDef>

</Defs>
